Close the *xattr(2) existence oracle on all four path_*xattr() wrappers.

  getxattr(p, "security.selinux")   ->  hands back the SELinux label
  listxattr(p, ...)                 ->  answers for a path stat() denies
  setxattr(p, ...)                  ->  -EROFS on a read-only ROM partition
  removexattr(p, ...)               ->  same

where a genuinely absent name gives -ENOENT to all four. The getxattr form was
measured on OP15 (Android 16, 6.12.23) against all 24 blocked uids with a
7020-path genuinely-absent control and zero false positives; the listxattr form
had already been measured leaking on this device (it answered for a uid that
stat() and open() denied).

WHY THE ENGINE CANNOT ANSWER IT

vfs_getxattr() routes anything under the "security." prefix to
xattr_getsecurity() -> security_inode_getsecurity(), answered out of the inode's
in-memory LSM blob -- the filesystem's own xattr handler is never reached, so
the hijacked inode_operations cannot intervene. xattr_permission() also returns
0 for security.* and system.* without calling inode_permission(), with the
comment "No restriction for security.* and system.* from the VFS." Verified
against v6.12 fs/xattr.c.

WHY ALL FOUR, NOT JUST getxattr

They are four independent probes of one bit. Closing getxattr alone leaves
three one-syscall replacements, so a partial cloak buys nothing while still
carrying all the risk of a cloak. Same reasoning as _hook's rule that a type
added to the hidden list has to be added at every call site.

WHERE THE GUARD GOES, AND WHY THERE

In each path_*xattr() syscall wrapper, immediately after user_path_at() returns
success -- not in vfs_getxattr() and friends, which in-kernel consumers
(overlayfs copy-up, nfsd, ecryptfs) reach in the calling task's context and
would therefore pass the uid gate.

For the placement argument -- why an EXISTENCE cloak belongs AHEAD of the
file-level permission check, which is the opposite of the rule common/_hook/
follows -- see the header of ghost_o_path.patch. Short version: for a name that
does not exist the kernel never performs a file-level check at all, and the
parent's search permission is still enforced because user_path_at() enforced it
before this guard runs.

f*xattr(2) (the fd forms) are deliberately NOT guarded: they need a descriptor
for the hidden path, and every route to one is closed already -- the engine's
hijacked ->open answers -ENOENT and ghost_o_path.patch closes the O_PATH route.

NOT applicable to 6.13+, where fs/xattr.c was restructured (path_getxattr()
became path_getxattrat() delegating to filename_getxattr(), and the wrappers
took a kernel_xattr_ctx). The equivalent 6.13+ sites are filename_getxattr() /
filename_listxattr() / filename_setxattr() / filename_removexattr(); no patch is
written for them because the builder fleet pins nothing above 6.12.

Hunk order follows file order: setxattr, getxattr, listxattr, removexattr.

APPLIES TO

Verified at fuzz 0 against vanilla v6.12 and against the pinned OnePlus
android15-6.6 and android16-6.12 trees. The OnePlus 6.6 tree carries the
6.12-era fs/xattr.c restructure (setxattr_copy()/xattr_ctx, do_setxattr(),
the kname buffer in path_removexattr()), so it groups HERE and not with
vanilla v6.6 -- which is exactly why ghost_xattr_6_6.patch exists separately.
